<h1 class="page-title">Feedra Prototype</h1>
<p class="standfirst">UX, web &amp; sound design &middot; 2025 &middot; for Pierre Proske</p>

<p>Feedra is a soundboard app for running live sound, whether it's a game master's tabletop campaign or a stage show: you build a scene's whole soundscape beforehand, then play it live. In 2025, electronic media artist and designer Pierre Proske was in the early stages of developing it, and wanted students' help brainstorming and prototyping ideas for its design and functionality.</p>
<p>Feedra was another collaboration with Nicholas Duncan (we'd previously worked on <a href="/sound-design/okiro/">Okiro</a> together). With Nick, I designed a website for the app's (hypothetical) promotion and distribution, and we worked out an overall look and set of conventions for the application. I also wrote thorough user documentation, and did the sound design for the demo scenes.</p>

{% video { youtube: "xR68Hf3g1fY", title: "Feedra cyberpunk stage show playthrough", caption: "A cyberpunk stage-show scene: a flying highway, city streets and a nightclub, with hovercars passing by, ID scans and a welcoming robot." } %}

<h2>The prototypes</h2>
<p>We designed the prototypes in Figma, from the first wireframes to a click-through, high-fidelity version, and built an interactive, low-fidelity version in Godot, with six demo scenes to play. In it, sounds are organised into campaigns and scenes, and each scene is a board of pads: big ones that loop (ambiences and music) and small ones for one-off effects, each with its own picture, volume, fades and effects.</p>

{% video { youtube: "vN-01wupS9s", title: "Feedra interactive prototype walkthrough", caption: "A walkthrough of the interactive prototype.", thumb: "sddefault" } %}

<h2>Live showcase</h2>
<p>At our showcase we ran a live game of Dungeons &amp; Dragons with Feedra (in the video below), as well as an interactive audio story set in a haunted house, and a live electronic music performance.</p>

{% video { youtube: "tLJQhvQUyLU", start: 768, title: "Feedra live showcase, 6 June 2025", caption: "Our live showcase on 6 June 2025." } %}

<h2>How we worked</h2>
<ul class="points">
  <li><b>Our own prototype.</b> We built ours completely separately from Feedra's own code. Pierre's version of the program was still very early in production, so he took inspiration from the direction we went in on our own, and helped guide our choices along the way.</li>
  <li><b>Every mode made useful.</b> Loops, one-shots, shuffling and random delays can combine into a mess, so we tried the combinations by ear with Pierre and turned the ones that worked into presets.</li>
  <li><b>Tested on people.</b> First-click tests and surveys showed people missing a pad's edit button and mixing up mute and delete, so the edit button got bigger and a label, mute became a speaker that changes colour, and delete moved away, with a confirmation. The website's feature names got plainer, too: "3D audio positioning", not "spatialisation".</li>
  <li><b>Agile.</b> A Kanban board in Notion, shared with Pierre, half-week sprints and a short retrospective every week.</li>
</ul>

